/*--------------------------------*- C++ -*----------------------------------*\
| needleDBD -- which mesh regions exist, and what KIND each one is            |
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
FoamFile
{
    version     2.0;
    format      ascii;
    class       dictionary;
    location    "constant";
    object      regionProperties;
}
// * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * //

// FORMAT:  regions ( <kind> ( <meshRegionName> ... ) ... );
//
// THE KIND IS A FIXED KEYWORD. THE NAME IS YOURS.
//
//   THREE kinds, looked up by literal string. You cannot invent or rename them:
//
//     gas         the plasma -- species, chemistry, electron energy, Poisson.
//                 Exactly one. epsilonR defaults to 1.0.
//     dielectric  a solid barrier -- Poisson only. Any number.
//                 epsilonR is REQUIRED, with no default.
//     farField    a FICTITIOUS air region -- Poisson only. Any number.
//                 epsilonR defaults to 1.0, which IS the definition of the kind.
//
//   `farField` exists to extend the ELECTROSTATIC domain without extending the
//   plasma one: Poisson is long-range and often needs a much larger domain than
//   the discharge, while species transport, chemistry and photoionization do
//   not. Wrapping a small gas region in a large farField region is therefore
//   nearly free. Not used in THIS case -- see the aside in TUTORIAL.md.
//
//   The names in parentheses are MESH region names -- the cellZone names, which
//   become the directory names under constant/ and system/. They are FREE:
//       dielectric  (plexiglass)      <- fine
//       gas         (leftGap)         <- fine
//   Here they happen to equal the kinds, which is convenient and not required;
//   the two-region electrostatics tutorial uses `gas (leftGas)`.
//
// RENAMING A REGION PROPAGATES, so know what it touches:
//   * constant/polyMesh/cellZones -- TWO places, the `meta { names ... }` list
//     AND the zone entry. Cleanest is to rename the gmsh `Physical Volume` and
//     re-import; otherwise edit both.
//   * constant/polyMesh/sets/<name> -- a gmshToFoam byproduct; rename the file
//     and its `object` header entry.
//   * THE INTERFACE PATCH NAMES, which are DERIVED as `<a>_to_<b>` --
//     `gas_to_plexiglass` and `plexiglass_to_gas`. Your etc/changeDictionary.*
//     boundary conditions name those patches explicitly, so a region rename
//     silently renames a patch you have to reference.
//
//   DO NOT `sed -i 's/dielectric/plexiglass/g' constant/polyMesh/*`. This mesh
//   has a PATCH called `air_dielectric` -- a gmsh physical curve, nothing to do
//   with the region -- and a global substitution renames it too, breaking the
//   boundary condition that names it.
//
// USING A WRONG KIND IS FATAL, deliberately. `plexiglass (plexiglass)` instead
// of `dielectric (plexiglass)` used to be SILENT: the kind matched nothing, the
// dielectric list came back empty, the model was derived as singleRegionPoisson,
// and the potential was solved in the gas alone -- a converged, plausible, wrong
// DBD. Guarded since 2026-09-02 in electromagneticsModel::validateRegionKinds.
// Note `foamListRegions` is no check: it flattens every list regardless of kind
// and would have printed both names quite happily.
//
// THIS FILE IS HAND-WRITTEN. Nothing generates it. splitMeshRegions writes
// constant/cellToRegion and the per-region polyMesh directories, but it does not
// know which region is the plasma and which is a solid -- that distinction is
// made here, and it decides what gets solved where.
//
// THIS FILE IS THE ONLY PLACE REGIONS ARE DECLARED. Everything else is derived
// from it:
//   * the Poisson model -- `multiRegionPoisson` when a `dielectric` is listed,
//     `singleRegionPoisson` when none is. It is not a user choice: both solve
//     the same equation and only the topology decides which applies.
//   * each region's properties -- read from constant/<region>/, so adding a
//     region means adding a DIRECTORY, and no global file is touched.
//
// A `dielectric` list MAY NAME AS MANY REGIONS AS YOU LIKE. Each one needs its
// own constant/<name>/electricalProperties with an `epsilonR`; there is no
// global file listing them.
//
// EXACTLY ONE `gas` REGION, though. That is not a limit on the geometry: a
// stack such as electrode|dielectric|gas|dielectric|gas|dielectric|electrode is
// ONE gas region whose cellZone is geometrically DISCONNECTED -- an OpenFOAM
// mesh region may consist of disjoint parts -- plus three dielectric regions.
// Every gap then shares one species set, one chemistry and one EEDF, which is
// what you want when the gaps hold the same gas. Two gas regions with DIFFERENT
// mixtures (air in one gap, CO2 in the other) is what is genuinely unsupported.
//
// THE NAMES MUST AGREE IN FIVE PLACES, or the split lands in directories nobody
// reads:
//   1. the gmsh `Physical Volume("gas")` / `Physical Volume("dielectric")`
//   2. the resulting cellZone names        (gmshToFoam prints these)
//   3. the parenthesised names here
//   4. the `constant/<region>/electricalProperties` directory names
//   5. the `etc/changeDictionary.<region>` filenames, and any loop over regions
//
// Without this file the solver falls back to a single default region and says
// so -- which for a two-region case means the dielectric is silently absent.

regions
(
    gas         (gas)
    dielectric  (dielectric)
);

// ************************************************************************* //
